<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Erskine Murray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Erskine Murray was a Scottish adventurer who in 1843 sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> hoping, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brooke">James Brooke</a> in Sarawak, to win a territory of his own. His attempt to gain trading concessions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Kutai on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakam-river">Mahakam River</a> ended in fighting in early 1844, in which he was killed. The incident prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to tighten their hold on eastern Borneo, and Kutai accepted Dutch overlordship that year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born James Murray in 1810, the seventh son of Baron Elibank by his second wife, he added Erskine to his name on marrying Isabella Erskine, a granddaughter of Lord Alva, in 1832. He became a lawyer at the Scottish bar. He wintered at Pau in 1834–1835, hunting there, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbes">Tarbes</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, then travelled by diligence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a> and came back on foot, recording and sketching Pyrenean customs, dialects and legends. He wrote an early travel journal of the French Pyrenees and an article on wolf hunting in the Landes, where villagers hunted on stilts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1841 he took his family, with two sons, two daughters and his younger brother Robert Dundas Murray, to Port Phillip in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Early in 1843 he left, ostensibly to trade, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, where he sold his ship Warlock and bought the 90-ton schooner Young Queen and the 200-ton brig Anna. With them he set out to found a settlement in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-of-sarawak">Rajah of Sarawak</a> had rebuffed his proposal to join that enterprise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Early in 1844 the two ships entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakam-river">Mahakam River</a>, then called the Kutai, and sailed up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenggarong">Tenggarong</a>, where Murray consulted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>. According to the survivors, the Sultan agreed to trade but would not let him settle, and the ships had to retreat under fire. Murray was killed, but both vessels escaped.</p><p>A survivor, Captain Hart, and others supplied the details for an account by William A. Gliddon, the younger brother of George Gliddon, and W. Cave Thomas later wrote the story up from another survivor's account. Historians have studied the episode in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, since it led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> sign a treaty acknowledging their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over Kutai, and in that of British involvement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Erskine Murray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a clash with British adventurer <b>James Erskine Murray</b>, the sultan accepted Dutch overlordship in 1844.</blockquote><a href="/state/kutai-kartanegara-sultanate">Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
